WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.360
Building modern mobile applications

2
00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:04.360
requires coordinating complex,

3
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:06.800
multi-threaded operating systems.

4
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.500
Commanding that native chaos

5
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.960
with a single threaded language like JavaScript

6
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.040
is an engineering nightmare.

7
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.520
In 2012, Facebook attempted to bypass this complexity

8
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.080
by wrapping their mobile web app

9
00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.600
inside a native web view shell.

10
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.480
The resulting interface felt sluggish

11
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.460
and failed to match the responsive feel

12
00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.260
of a native application.

13
00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.440
The solution arrived in 2015

14
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.680
with the launch of React Native.

15
00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:32.320
This new approach allowed developers

16
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.040
to write logic in JavaScript

17
00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.460
that rendered actual native OS primitives

18
00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.480
instead of slow HTML.

19
00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.880
This solved platform fragmentation.

20
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.160
To build the same layout,

21
00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.680
web developers write a JSX div,

22
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:47.560
iOS engineers write a SwiftUI VStack,

23
00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.520
and Android developers write Kotlin XML.

24
00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.760
React Native unified these isolated ecosystems.

25
00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.160
A single JavaScript view component

26
00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:57.860
maps directly to the UI primitive

27
00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:59.980
required by the host OS.

28
00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:01.860
Learn once, write anywhere

29
00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.540
delivered a productive developer experience.

30
00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:05.620
But this translation layer

31
00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:08.180
introduced a severe architectural compromise

32
00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:09.840
that limited the framework's performance

33
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.620
for nearly a decade.

34
00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:12.740
For seven years,

35
00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.520
this compromise artificially capped performance.

36
00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.140
Complex applications hit a strict speed limit,

37
00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.680
driving many engineering teams to abandon the framework

38
00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.100
in search of pure native speeds.

39
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.340
That ceiling has now been removed.

40
00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:26.900
In October, 2024,

41
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:30.380
React Native 0.76 arrived with a ground up rewrite

42
00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.220
of its internal architecture.

43
00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:33.220
Meta and its partners

44
00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.540
swapped the framework's internal foundation,

45
00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.260
removing the original memory and communication layers

46
00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.960
to install a new C++ core.

47
00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.340
By replacing the old JSON message bridge

48
00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:45.540
with a shared C++ memory model,

49
00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:48.460
the framework can now execute pure native operations

50
00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:51.500
at a flawless 120 frames per second.

51
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.540
To understand how these speeds are possible,

52
00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.180
we must first reverse engineer

53
00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:57.360
exactly why the original architecture

54
00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.100
collapsed under heavy load.

55
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.520
This schematic illustrates

56
00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.900
the legacy React Native architecture.

57
00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.540
The JavaScript thread where your app logic lives

58
00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:08.100
is totally isolated from the native thread,

59
00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:09.860
which renders the interface.

60
00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.420
Every user interaction,

61
00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:13.540
measuring a layout, drawing a pixel,

62
00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:14.940
or capturing a swipe,

63
00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:16.980
had to traverse this gap.

64
00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:20.680
The mechanism connecting them was known as the bridge.

65
00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.060
To cross this divide,

66
00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:25.980
every native command was serialized into a JSON text string,

67
00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.380
queued up, and then deserialized on the other side.

68
00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:31.940
Because this bridge was strictly asynchronous,

69
00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:32.820
the JavaScript thread

70
00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:35.300
could never read native values instantly.

71
00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.820
If the code needed to know the exact dimensions

72
00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:39.260
of a view on the screen,

73
00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:42.280
it had to send a request and wait for a reply.

74
00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:44.160
Under low load, this worked.

75
00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.500
But during a complex animation at 60 frames per second,

76
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:50.460
the volume of layout data overwhelms the queue.

77
00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:54.140
Serialized messages backup, stall, and eventually drop.

78
00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.900
The JSON bridge acted as a rigid choke point,

79
00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:59.180
creating inherent latency for any operation

80
00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:02.100
that required synchronous UI updates.

81
00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:05.320
React Native 0.76 solves this bottleneck

82
00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:06.580
by removing the bridge

83
00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:10.660
and replacing it with the JavaScript interface, or JSI.

84
00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:14.660
JSI alters how memory is accessed at a structural level.

85
00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:16.300
The Hermes JavaScript engine

86
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:20.420
now holds direct references to native C++ objects.

87
00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:23.140
Since these environments now share the same memory space,

88
00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:25.460
the system no longer needs to translate commands

89
00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:27.420
into JSON text strings.

90
00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.140
JavaScript can now invoke methods

91
00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:32.020
on native objects strictly synchronously.

92
00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:34.020
If your logic needs of used dimensions,

93
00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:36.580
it reads the C++ object directly.

94
00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:39.100
This instant access eliminates the asynchronous

95
00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:42.180
waiting period that defined the old queue system.

96
00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.280
With JSI, React Native functions

97
00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.420
as a synchronous execution engine,

98
00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:49.780
operating with the speed of direct C++ memory access.

99
00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.060
To take advantage of this direct memory access,

100
00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:55.060
the engineering teams rebuilt the rest

101
00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.720
of the framework's core systems from scratch.

102
00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.800
The resulting architecture is built on three pillars.

103
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.380
The first is Fabric,

104
00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:06.260
a new UI rendering engine built natively in C++.

105
00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:07.620
Fabric enables the framework

106
00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:10.140
to calculate layout measurements synchronously,

107
00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:11.620
ensuring the UI is ready

108
00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:14.060
before it is ever drawn to the screen.

109
00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:16.220
The second pillar is Turbo Modules.

110
00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:19.660
The modern replacement for the legacy native module system.

111
00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:21.860
Instead of initializing hardware modules

112
00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:23.420
like the camera or Bluetooth

113
00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:25.100
during the app's boot sequence,

114
00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:28.140
Turbo Modules are loaded into memory via JSI

115
00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:30.180
only when they are actually requested,

116
00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:32.180
reducing startup times.

117
00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:34.980
The combined architecture of JSI, Fabric,

118
00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.320
and Turbo Modules ensures that the boundary

119
00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.260
between JavaScript and native code

120
00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:42.140
no longer carries a performance penalty.

121
00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:45.380
This C++ foundation changes the user experience

122
00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:46.460
by enabling features

123
00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:49.100
that were previously impossible on mobile.

124
00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:51.540
On the web, React concurrent mode features

125
00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.460
like Suspense and Transitions

126
00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:56.300
allow the framework to prioritize urgent updates.

127
00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:58.060
The old asynchronous architecture

128
00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:00.540
could not handle these features reliably.

129
00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:03.960
On the left, the old asynchronous bridge fails to keep up.

130
00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:06.620
Rendering tasks fall out of sync with the native UI,

131
00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:08.220
causing dropped frames.

132
00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.680
On the right, the Fabric engine executes concurrently.

133
00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:13.740
Synchronous communication allows the UI

134
00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:17.340
to pause and resume tasks without blocking the main thread.

135
00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.980
This same mechanism powers modern animation libraries

136
00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:21.540
like Reanimated3.

137
00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:23.620
Since layout measurements are now synchronous,

138
00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:26.580
animations execute entirely on the native UI thread

139
00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:28.900
at up to 120 frames per second,

140
00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.860
with no traffic crossing back into JavaScript.

141
00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:33.980
Developers are no longer forced to choose

142
00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:36.140
between advanced React rendering features

143
00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:38.380
and pure native performance.

144
00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:39.720
Mobile development has moved

145
00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:41.780
from the sluggish web views of 2012

146
00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:46.140
to the high-performance C++ core of version 0.76.

147
00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:48.620
The widespread assumption that React Native is slow

148
00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:49.980
is based on an architecture

149
00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:52.460
that effectively no longer exists.

150
00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:54.140
The core philosophy remains,

151
00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:56.540
write TypeScript once and ship native UI

152
00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:58.020
to both platforms.

153
00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:00.460
The difference now is that you can do so

154
00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:04.220
without paying the hidden tax of the JSON bridge.

155
00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:06.940
By solving the multi-threading communication bottleneck

156
00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:08.300
at the memory level,

157
00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.640
React Native secures its position

158
00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:13.640
as the framework for the future of mobile development.

